  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and the rule reads back as enabled.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) has the time field `timestamp`. The response code is a string field, `response`, with a `response.keyword` subfield, which I used for aggregation and filtering.\n\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-24b0b5e9 Web logs' (id `s4-24b0b5e9-web-logs-5eac2db72de2`) has two panels:\n   - Requests over time: a line chart of count by `timestamp` date histogram.\n   - Requests by response code: a bar chart of count by `response.keyword`, top 10.\n\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-24b0b5e9 5xx errors' (id `3f8260ec-02be-429b-8272-4dd9ac24070a`) is an `.es-query` rule on `stackAlerts`, scheduled every 1m.\n   - The query matches `response.keyword` from \"500\" to \"599\". This is a lexicographic range, which matches numeric order because every code is three digits.\n   - It fires when the count is greater than 10 over a 5-minute window.\n   - The rule was created disabled, so I enabled it.\n\n4. **Panel edit:** The response-code panel is now titled 'Responses by code'. I re-sent its full spec with the new title, since the update replaces the panel. The dashboard shows both titles as expected. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true`, with status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
